"[2511.08835v1] Beyond Task-Oriented and Chitchat Dialogues: Proactive and Transition-Aware Conversational Agents (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08835v1)\nciteturn5929view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08835v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 163\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 11 Nov 2025]\nL9: # Title:Beyond Task-Oriented and Chitchat Dialogues: Proactive and Transition-Aware Conversational Agents\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Yejin Yoon , cite6†Yuri Son , cite7†Namyoung So , cite8†Minseo Kim , cite9†Minsoo Cho , cite10†Chanhee Park , cite11†Seungshin Lee , cite12†Taeuk Kim L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Beyond Task-Oriented and Chitchat Dialogues: Proactive and Transition-Aware Conversational Agents, by Yejin Yoon and 7 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite13†View PDF cite14†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Conversational agents have traditionally been developed for either task-oriented dialogue (TOD) or open-ended chitchat, with limited progress in unifying the two. Yet, real-world conversations naturally involve fluid transitions between these modes. To address this gap, we introduce TACT (TOD-And-Chitchat Transition), a dataset designed for transition-aware dialogue modeling that incorporates structurally diverse and integrated mode flows.\nL17: TACT supports both user- and agent-driven mode switches, enabling robust modeling of complex conversational dynamics. To evaluate an agent's ability to initiate and recover from mode transitions, we propose two new metrics -- Switch and Recovery. Models trained on TACT outperform baselines in both intent detection and mode transition handling.\nL18: Moreover, applying Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) to TACT-trained models yields additional gains, achieving 75.74\\% joint mode-intent accuracy and a 70.1\\% win rate against GPT-4o in human evaluation. These results demonstrate that pairing structurally diverse data with DPO enhances response quality and transition control, paving the way for more proactive and transition-aware conversational agents.\nL19: Comments:  | accepted to EMNLP2025\nL20: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite15†arXiv:2511.08835 [cs.CL]\nL22:    | (or cite16†arXiv:2511.08835v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL23:    | cite17†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.08835†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: Journal reference:  | Proceedings of the 2025 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP 2025), pages 13291-13317, Suzhou, China. Association for Computational Linguistics\nL25: Related DOI (Digital Object Identifier):  | cite18†https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2025.emnlp-main.672†doi.org DOI(s) linking to related resources\nL26: ## Submission history\nL27: \nL28: From: Yejin Yoon [cite19†view email ]\nL29: [v1] Tue, 11 Nov 2025 23:03:44 UTC (10,111 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled Beyond Task-Oriented and Chitchat Dialogues: Proactive and Transition-Aware Conversational Agents, by Yejin Yoon and 7 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * cite13†View PDF L38:   * cite14†HTML (experimental) L39:   * cite20†TeX Source L40: \nL41: cite21†view license†creativecommons.org L42: ### Current browse context:\nL43: \nL44: cs.CL\nL45: \nL46: cite22†< prev |   cite23†next > L47: \nL48: cite24†new | cite25†recent | cite26†2025-11 L49: \nL50: Change to browse by:\nL51: \nL52: cite27†cs L53: cite28†cs.AI L54: \nL55: ### References & Citations\nL56: \nL57:   * cite