: 200] Crawled: 10 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08916v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 201\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:  cite57†Image: Cornell University L3: \nL4:  We gratefully acknowledge support from the Simons Foundation, cite1†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , and all contributors. cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org L5: \nL6: cite58†Image: arxiv logo > cite3†cs > arXiv:2511.08916v1\nL7: \nL8: cite4†Help†info.arxiv.org | cite5†Advanced Search L9: \nL10:  All fields Title Author Abstract Comments Journal reference ACM classification MSC classification Report number arXiv identifier DOI ORCID arXiv author ID Help pages Full text\nL11: \nL12: Search\nL13: cite59†Image: arXiv logo L14: \nL15: cite6† †www.cornell.edu L16: \nL17:  open search\nL18: \nL19: GO\nL20: \nL21: open navigation menu\nL22: ## quick links\nL23: \nL24:   * cite7†Login L25:   * cite4†Help Pages†info.arxiv.org L26:   * cite8†About†info.arxiv.org L27: \nL28: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL29: \nL30: arXiv:2511.08916v1 (cs)\nL31: \nL32: [Submitted on 12 Nov 2025 (this version), latest version 20 Nov 2025 (cite9†v3 )]\nL33: # Title:HalluClean: A Unified Framework to Combat Hallucinations in LLMs\nL34: \nL35: Authors:cite10†Yaxin Zhao , cite11†Yu Zhang L36: \nL37: View a PDF of the paper titled HalluClean: A Unified Framework to Combat Hallucinations in LLMs, by Yaxin Zhao and 1 other authors\nL38: \nL39: cite12†View PDF cite13†HTML (experimental) L40: > Abstract:Large language models (LLMs) have achieved impressive performance across a wide range of natural language processing tasks, yet they often produce hallucinated content that undermines factual reliability. To address this challenge, we introduce HalluClean, a lightweight and task-agnostic framework for detecting and correcting hallucinations in LLM-generated text.\nL41: HalluClean adopts a reasoning-enhanced paradigm, explicitly decomposing the process into planning, execution, and revision stages to identify and refine unsupported claims. It employs minimal task-routing prompts to enable zero-shot generalization across diverse domains, without relying on external knowledge sources or supervised detectors. We conduct extensive evaluations on five representative tasks-question answering, dialogue, summarization, math word problems, and contradiction detection.\nL42: Experimental results show that HalluClean significantly improves factual consistency and outperforms competitive baselines, demonstrating its potential to enhance the trustworthiness of LLM outputs in real-world applications.\nL43: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL44: Cite as: cite14†arXiv:2511.08916 [cs.CL]\nL45:   (or  cite15†arXiv:2511.08916v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL46:   cite16†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.08916†doi.org L47: \nL48: Focus to learn more\nL49: \nL50:   arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL51: ## Submission history\nL52: \nL53: From: Yaxin Zhao [cite17†view email ]\nL54: [v1] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 02:50:56 UTC (1,897 KB)\nL55: cite18†[v2] Wed, 19 Nov 2025 08:22:50 UTC (1,898 KB)\nL56: cite9†[v3] Thu, 20 Nov 2025 11:42:04 UTC (1,898 KB)\nL57: \nL58: Full-text links:\nL59: ## Access Paper:\nL60: \nL61:  View a PDF of the paper titled HalluClean: A Unified Framework to Combat Hallucinations in LLMs, by Yaxin Zhao and 1 other authors\nL62: \nL63:   * cite12†View PDF L64:   * cite13†HTML (experimental) L65:   * cite19†TeX Source  L66: \nL67: cite20†view license L68: \nL69: Current browse context:\nL70: \nL71: cs.CL\nL72: \nL73: cite21†< prev |   cite22†next > L74: \nL75: cite23†new |  cite24†recent | cite25†2025-11 L76: \nL77: Change to browse by:\nL78: \nL79: cite26†cs L80: ### References & Citations\nL81: \nL82:   * cite27†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L83:   * cite28†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L84:   * cite29